1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Power systems engineering is a lot of math:

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:09,109
complex power, per-unit bases, sequence
networks, iterative solvers.

3
00:00:09,209 --> 00:00:13,842
This calculator puts the whole toolkit in a
single browser page.

4
00:00:13,942 --> 00:00:22,273
Six modules, live diagrams that update as
you type, and a theory page that shows the
formulas behind every result.

5
00:00:22,373 --> 00:00:23,900
Let's dive in!

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,736
First, the basics.

7
00:00:27,836 --> 00:00:34,564
A plant draws 500 kilowatts at 0.75
lagging, on a 480-volt system.

8
00:00:34,664 --> 00:00:40,071
That low power factor means much of the
current does no useful work.

9
00:00:40,171 --> 00:00:43,008
The calculator draws the power triangle:

10
00:00:43,108 --> 00:00:46,900
real power P, reactive Q, and apparent S.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,746
Here, Q is 441 kilovars, and S is 667 kVA.

12
00:00:51,846 --> 00:00:54,463
Now correct power factor to 0.95.

13
00:00:54,563 --> 00:00:59,823
The formula subtracts the tangents of the
two angles and multiplies by P.

14
00:00:59,923 --> 00:01:03,715
That takes a capacitor bank of 277 kilovars.

15
00:01:03,815 --> 00:01:15,096
Apparent power drops to about 526 kilovolt-
amperes, and line current falls by roughly
twenty-one percent, freeing capacity and
avoiding utility surcharges.

16
00:01:15,196 --> 00:01:28,900
Switch between wye and delta, and the line
and phase quantities update, including the
square-root-of-three relationships and the
divide-by-three rule for converting delta
impedance to wye.

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,420
Next, the generator!

18
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:40,174
This module solves for the internal voltage
and the torque angle delta, and plots power
against delta.

19
00:01:40,274 --> 00:01:47,015
For a simple round-rotor machine, power
follows a sine curve that peaks at ninety
degrees.

20
00:01:47,115 --> 00:01:50,512
That peak is the steady-state stability
limit.

21
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:55,909
A salient-pole machine, like those in hydro
plants, adds a second term:

22
00:01:56,009 --> 00:02:00,014
reluctance power varying with sine of two delta.

23
00:02:00,114 --> 00:02:09,972
Here it adds about twenty-six megawatts at
its peak, and it moves the stability limit
down to sixty-six degrees, at 96.4
megawatts.

24
00:02:10,072 --> 00:02:17,270
The operating point sits at 22.7 degrees and
50 megawatts, leaving about 46 megawatts of
margin.

25
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:20,918
Push the load up, and the point climbs the curve.

26
00:02:21,018 --> 00:02:28,900
Cross the limit, and the rotor slips poles
and loses synchronism, which puts violent
torque on the shaft.

27
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:31,798
Transformers!

28
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:37,465
Shop load-testing isn't practical, so engineers run two simple tests.

29
00:02:37,565 --> 00:02:42,371
The open-circuit test, on the low-voltage
side, gives the shunt branch:

30
00:02:42,471 --> 00:02:44,858
core loss and magnetizing reactance.

31
00:02:44,958 --> 00:02:49,972
The short-circuit test, on the high-voltage
side, gives the series branch:

32
00:02:50,072 --> 00:02:52,805
winding resistance and leakage reactance.

33
00:02:52,905 --> 00:03:02,064
Enter the readings and the app extracts all
four values and draws an approximate
equivalent circuit, referred to the low-
voltage side.

34
00:03:02,164 --> 00:03:09,527
From it we get full-load efficiency of 98.03
percent at 0.8 lagging, and voltage
regulation of 1.94 percent.

35
00:03:09,627 --> 00:03:16,023
Regulation matters because leakage reactance
drops the secondary voltage under inductive
load.

36
00:03:16,123 --> 00:03:23,900
And efficiency matters because a transformer
runs around the clock for decades, so even a
small core loss adds up.

37
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,332
Transmission lines!

38
00:03:27,432 --> 00:03:29,065
Model choice by length:

39
00:03:29,165 --> 00:03:34,416
short under 80 km, medium to 250, and long above that.

40
00:03:34,516 --> 00:03:37,882
These are typical guidelines, not hard
limits.

41
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:47,151
For long lines, hyperbolic equations give the ABCD parameters.

42
00:03:47,251 --> 00:03:51,522
On this 300-kilometer line, the long model is active.

43
00:03:51,622 --> 00:03:54,838
The graph shows the no-load voltage profile.

44
00:03:54,938 --> 00:04:04,785
Voltage rises from 281.6 kilovolts sending to 303 at the far end, a 21.3 kV rise.

45
00:04:04,885 --> 00:04:09,683
That's the Ferranti effect, caused by the
line's own capacitance.

46
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:13,075
The surge impedance loading is 126
megawatts:

47
00:04:13,175 --> 00:04:17,144
the natural loading where the profile is
ideally flat.

48
00:04:17,244 --> 00:04:20,234
The same tab handles per-unit conversion.

49
00:04:20,334 --> 00:04:28,900
A base impedance removes the transformer
ratios, so a system spanning several voltage
levels solves as one network.

50
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,751
Power flow answers the grid operator's
question:

51
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:41,810
given where power is generated and consumed,
what are the voltages and flows everywhere?

52
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:44,056
This is a three-bus network.

53
00:04:44,156 --> 00:04:45,901
Bus 1 is the slack bus,

54
00:04:46,001 --> 00:04:49,993
balancing the grid to fix voltage and angle.

55
00:04:50,093 --> 00:04:55,688
Bus 2 is a generator with fixed output, 50
megawatts, at 1.03 per unit.

56
00:04:55,788 --> 00:04:59,539
Bus 3 draws 80 megawatts and 40 megavars.

57
00:04:59,639 --> 00:05:11,171
The app builds the bus admittance matrix and
solves with Gauss-Seidel, updating each bus
voltage until the change drops below one in
ten million.

58
00:05:11,271 --> 00:05:16,626
It converges in ten iterations, and Bus 3
settles at 0.965 per unit.

59
00:05:16,726 --> 00:05:22,964
The slack generator supplies 33.2 megawatts,
and line losses are 3.2 megawatts.

60
00:05:23,064 --> 00:05:28,900
Increase the load on Bus 3 and watch the
voltage sag and the losses climb.

61
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,269
Finally, faults!

62
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:40,310
Real faults are usually unbalanced, so the
calculator uses Fortescue's symmetrical
components:

63
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:47,581
any unbalanced set of three phasors splits
into positive, negative and zero sequence.

64
00:05:47,681 --> 00:05:53,227
Pick the fault type and the diagram rewires
the networks to match.

65
00:05:53,327 --> 00:06:01,696
A single line-to-ground fault connects all
three in series, giving 4.29 per unit, or
1.79 kiloamps.

66
00:06:01,796 --> 00:06:06,145
A three-phase fault uses positive sequence:

67
00:06:06,245 --> 00:06:07,342
giving 5 per unit.

68
00:06:07,442 --> 00:06:14,614
A line-to-line fault puts positive and negative in parallel:

69
00:06:14,714 --> 00:06:15,812
giving 4.33 per unit.

70
00:06:15,912 --> 00:06:22,399
For a double line-to-ground fault, ground current is:

71
00:06:22,499 --> 00:06:23,596
3.75 per unit.

72
00:06:23,696 --> 00:06:28,900
These numbers size circuit breakers and set
protective relays.

73
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,333
Every result in the calculator ties back to
this page.

74
00:06:36,433 --> 00:06:50,418
The theory guide explains each model,
numbers every equation, and compares star
and delta connections and the four fault
types side by side.

75
00:06:50,518 --> 00:07:03,900
It follows standard references, including
Glover, Grainger and Stevenson, and Saadat,
so you can check the work against the
textbooks.

76
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:14,706
Six modules, live diagrams, verified math,
and the theory beside it.

77
00:07:14,806 --> 00:07:23,900
Open the calculator, change a number, and
see what happens to the grid.
